nd recording N diagnostics / M files.\n- Docs per layer, same commit series: `docs/code-quality-tracker.md` (Current submission row, inventory number chain continuation, rule-table state flip when enabling a rule) and `CHANGELOG.md` (dated entry, newest-first). `pnpm exec prettier --write` on edited markdown + `pnpm lint:markdown` before committing.\n- Conventional commits (`refactor(api): …`, `chore(quality): …`), each ending with:\n  Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n  Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AboAw4ZrbW6qs7dQkqBf8C\n\n## Resource bounds (WSL2 laptop)\n- Sequential workspace builds only; bounded test workers; NO local Playwright Product E2E ever (CI owns it); API integration tests need Docker Postgres (`pnpm db:up`, `pnpm db:migrate`, `pnpm db:provision-rls`) — if Docker or the port is unavailable, say so explicitly, never skip silently.\n- Never claim a check passed without running it and seeing it pass.\n\n## Git discipline (hard rules)\n- Work only in your worktree on your stack branches. Never touch master, never force-push except `gh stack` doing so internally, never `git stash` / `reset --hard` / `checkout -- .`.\n- Commit early and often — make work durable before long verification runs.\n\n## Cadence\nAfter every 2–3 completed layers: `gh stack submit --auto --open`, fix titles/bodies, then RETURN to your orchestrator with a status report — layers completed, PR numbers, inventory before→after per layer, next planned boundary. Do not exhaust your context finishing everything; submit and report instead.\n\nStart now: fetch, read the docs, measure the inventory, init the stack, and build the first layer.",
